    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499914/assistant-dean-and-chief-operations-officer": {
      "title": "Assistant Dean and Chief Operations Officer",
      "description": "Assistant Dean and Chief Operations Officer Apply now Search #: 499914 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Executive JOB SUMMARY The School of Fine Arts at the University of Connecticut invites applications for the position of Assistant Dean and Chief Operations Officer (COO), reporting to the Dean of the School. The Assistant Dean/COO serves as a principal advisor to the Dean, Associate Deans, Department Heads, and Directors with regards to strategic planning, resource allocation, financial stewardship, organizational effectiveness, personnel management, space planning, compliance and operational continuity within a highly decentralized environment. As a member of the Dean’s senior leadership team, the AD/COO helps establish organizational priorities, guide operational decision-making, and align resources with the school’s mission. The position provides direct oversight of school-level finance, human resources, academic personnel, payroll, facilities, and information technology activities, while leading budgeting, organizational strategies, and implementation of operational priorities. This role combines strategic leadership with hands-on operational management, requiring strong collaboration, clear direction, effective conflict resolution, and proactive management of operational, financial, and personnel matters. This position is based at the Storrs campus but also covers School of Fine Arts operations and programs at UConn regional campuses. Occasional evenings, weekends, and in-state travel may be required. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES In consultation with the Dean, develops, evaluates, interprets, and enforces university and School policies, procedures, guidelines, and strategies. Oversees fiscal management functions, including budgeting, financial analysis, reporting, audits, internal controls and centralization of financial operations for all academic departments, centers, venues, revenue-generating programs, and UConn Foundation partnerships within the school. Supervises key professional staff in finance and operations with dotted line supervision of information technology personnel Leads human resources functions, including managing staffing processes, liaising with Labor Relations and Human Resources, coordinating performance evaluations, and ensuring compliance. Provides direction over the School of Fine Arts information technology services, including strategic planning, technology upgrades, and data management, in consultation and collaboration with centralized ITS as needed. Oversees facilities and space management across buildings and assigned spaces of the School of Fine Arts. Collaborates with university general counsel to manage the school’s legal risk, ensuring adherence to regulatory standards, and overseeing the execution of institutional agreements Oversees School of Fine Arts relationships with university partner offices (including Provost, Budget Office, OVPR, Bursar, Purchasing, Tax and Compliance Offices and Travel). Represents the School of Fine Arts on university committees and leadership forums when called on by the Dean. Performs other duties as required. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree in business administration, accounting, human resources, public administration, higher education administration, arts administration, or related field or bachelor’s degree and equivalent work experience. 7 or more years of progressively responsible experience in higher education administration or a similar complex organization. Demonstrated experience with fiscal oversight, budget development, and financial analysis. Supervisory experience managing professional staff and cross-functional teams. Demonstrated knowledge of relevant federal, state, and university regulations governing HR, finance, and business operations. Demonstrated communication, problem-solving, and policy interpretation skills. Experience with human resource management, labor relations, staff evaluations, and/or organizational development. Experience in strategic planning, organizational process improvement, or operational efficiency initiatives. Proven ability to work collaboratively with diverse stakeholders PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS 2 or more years of experience in higher education leadership. 6 or more years of experience in higher education leadership with a fiscal/financial role. Proven experience using data/financial reporting to inform decisions. Demonstrated experience managing complex contracts, legal compliance, or risk management, preferably within a higher education or public sector environment. Experience managing facilities operations, space planning, or capital improvement planning. Experience in a unionized environment. Experience with RCM Budget Models. Experience with arts organizations. COMPENSATION & BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $142,000 - $155,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is considered management/confidential and look for information specifically identified for management/confidential, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, permanent, 12-month, management-exempt position with an anticipated start date in January or February 2027. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499914 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on November 30, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 29 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 30 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499834/itsle-operations-analyst": {
      "title": "IT-SLE Operations Analyst",
      "description": "IT-SLE Operations Analyst Apply now Search #: 499834 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Administrative Services JOB SUMMARY Serves as the bridge between business intelligence, data analysis, advanced analytics, and innovative AI solutions, incorporating AI into daily analytical processes and decision-making for operational teams. Performs data analysis and modeling to identify trends, patterns, opportunities, and solutions to operational and business problems, while leveraging artificial intelligence tools, automation, and advanced analytics to improve data exploration, statistical models, generate actionable insights, and support decision-making. This position leverages the practical integration of AI into existing operational workflows. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Assess and Identify Areas of Opportunity for AI Identify the standard operational workflows, systems, and processes for an operational team and assess opportunities where AI may support efficiencies in workflow, data collection, analysis, and decision-making. Assess operational workflows, systems, and analytical processes to identify inefficiencies and opportunities for AI, automation, and process improvement, reducing manual effort, improving turnaround time, or enhancing analytical consistency. Perform ad hoc and recurring data analysis and modeling to address operational questions, identify trends and patterns, evaluate performance, and support decision-making. Collect, prepare, evaluate, and analyze data from multiple sources to identify opportunities, assess operational performance, and develop actionable insights. Meet with operational partners to determine feasibility of applying AI‑assisted tools (e.g., copilots, large language models, automated analytics) to support data exploration, modeling, insight generation, and analytical efficiency. Develop and Implement AI Solutions Partner with analysts to introduce AI‑enabled methods into standard analytical practices. Translate business questions into AI‑assisted analytical approaches, including natural‑language querying, statistical analysis, model interpretation, pattern detection, and predictive or other analytical modeling. Prototype and test AI‑enabled analytical methods that augment existing reporting, dashboards, and analysis workflows. Develop low‑code or no‑code automations within existing software that streamline routine data preparation, analysis, and reporting tasks. Apply and interpret statistical, mathematical, or predictive models, as appropriate, to support operational planning, evaluation, forecasting, and decision-making. Collaborate with data engineers or platform teams to operationalize repeatable analytical processes. Validate AI outputs for accuracy, bias, and relevance before use in decision‑making. Integrate AI-supported reporting, trend detection, anomaly identification, and analytical modeling to support dashboards, reports, and ad‑hoc analyses. Communication and Stewardship of AI Solutions Collaborate with stakeholders to explain AI‑supported insights, analytical findings, and modeling results in clear, non‑technical language. Serve as a resource for responsible AI use, helping analysts understand appropriate use cases and limitations. Follow organizational standards for data governance, privacy, and responsible AI use. Document AI‑assisted workflows, analytical methodologies, modeling assumptions, and sources to ensure transparency and reproducibility. Stay informed on emerging AI tools and techniques relevant to data analysis and modeling, recommending adoption when value‑aligned. Supports technical projects as part of a project team. Evaluate internal systems for efficiency, problems, and inaccuracies. Performs related work as required MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in information technology, business administration, data analysis or related field, and six years of related experience; or a master’s degree and four years of related experience. Experience applying AI tools to improve real-world workflows (e.g., analysis, drafting, summarization, customer support, knowledge search, or automation). Experience performing statistical data analysis and modeling using large datasets, with practical experience in understanding their use in making informed decisions. Experience evaluating and operating AI solutions (test cases/metrics, monitoring/logging) with a strong grounding in responsible AI (privacy, security, bias, accessibility). Experience taking an AI-enabled workflow from prototype to pilot/production, including stakeholder feedback, documentation, and basic automation/API integration. Ability to design, iterate, and document prompts/workflows; validate AI outputs for accuracy, bias, and appropriateness before use. Ability to translate stakeholder needs into clear problem statements, success criteria, and practical AI-enabled solutions. Working knowledge of AI/ML and generative AI fundamentals, including common limitations and risks (e.g., model evaluation basics, hallucinations, and prompt sensitivity). Proficiency with at least one scripting or automation approach (e.g., Python, SQL, PowerShell, R, or a low-code platform) to streamline and standardize processes. Knowledge of responsible AI, privacy, and security practices, including appropriate handling of sensitive data when using AI systems. Knowledge of statistical models, data analysis techniques, predictive modeling, and/or optimization. Experience communicating analytical findings to non-technical stakeholders. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Hands-on experience building AI-assisted solutions with large language models (LLMs), including prompt/workflow design and structured outputs. Familiarity with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), semantic search, embeddings, and working with unstructured content (documents/knowledge bases). Experience applying advanced analytical, statistical, predictive, or mathematical modeling techniques to evaluate operational performance, identify trends and patterns, and develop data-driven recommendations. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $85,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA) and look for information specifically identified for UCPEA, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, permanent position. The University offers a competitive salary, and outstanding benefits, including employee and dependent tuition waivers at UConn, and a highly desirable work environment. For additional information regarding benefits, please visit UConn Health Benefits and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves and Engagement & Learning headers. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499834 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on November 6, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 29 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 06 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499879/administrative-program-support-2": {
      "title": "Administrative Program Support 2",
      "description": "Administrative Program Support 2 Apply now Search #: 499879 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Administrative Services JOB SUMMARY The School of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences at the University of Connecticut seeks candidates for a full-time Administrative Program Support 2 (P2) position within the Department of Pharmacy Practice (DPP) and the Business Office. Under the direction of the Assistant Dean for Finance and Operations, this position will provide program, operational and some administrative support to the department head, the faculty, staff, adjunct faculty, and other departmental constituents across a broad range of academic, personnel, financial, contractual, operational, and compliance-related activities. This position coordinates complex departmental processes involving faculty located at the Storrs campus and at numerous clinical and practice sites throughout Connecticut. The incumbent serves as a primary departmental liaison with University offices, external clinical and practice sites, faculty committees and other stakeholders. The position requires substantial independent judgement, discretion, problem-solving, organization, and follow-through. The incumbent must interpret and apply University policies and procedures, coordinate multiple concurrent processes and deadlines, maintain confidential records, identify and resolve administrative issues, and ensure that departmental activities are completed accurately and in compliance with University policies and procedures. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Coordinates faculty review, evaluation, and development processes, including annual reports, merit reviews, workload reporting, Individual Development Plans (IDPs), annual performance evaluations, and monthly faculty meetings; tracks timelines and completion. Collects, validates, analyzes, and reports faculty merit and workload information; maintains records and prepares reports and supports documentation. Coordinates the Department's promotion, tenure, and reappointment (P(T)R) process by tracking requirements, timelines, review activities, and submission of materials to the Dean's Office. Serves as a resource to full-time, part-time, adjunct, and volunteer faculty regarding University and departmental policies, procedures, deadlines, and faculty-related processes. Coordinates appointment, hiring, and employment processes for adjunct, volunteer, and other non-regular faculty, including instructional appointments, Special Payroll transactions, Dual Employment requests, and joint appointments; prepares documentation and monitors approvals. Supports faculty recruitment and separation activities, including search logistics, candidate communications, onboarding and offboarding, records management, equipment recovery, and related personnel transactions. Coordinates agreements and affiliations with external practice sites and partners, including MOUs, contracts, and affiliation agreements; tracks renewals, documentation, and compliance requirements. Serves as a liaison among faculty, external practice sites, and University offices to facilitate communication, resolve issues, and support compliance and operational needs. Coordinates operational support for faculty and departmental activities at the Storrs campus and external practice sites, including workspace, technology, equipment, access, and other resources. Maintains departmental records, databases, inventories, and tracking systems related to faculty activities, University assets, compliance requirements, and departmental operations. Provides support for departmental purchasing and travel activities, including ProCard transactions, procurement, travel reimbursements, and documentation review; maintains related financial records. Coordinates departmental meetings, events, and special projects, including scheduling, communications, agendas, meeting materials, facilities, technology, parking, and other logistics. Manages departmental communications and information distribution through approved University channels; maintains contact and distribution lists, responds to inquiries, routes correspondence, and serves as a resource for faculty, staff, MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree and two years of related experience or associate’s degree and three to four years of professional-based experience. At least two years of administrative experience. Experience with Microsoft Office Suite, including Excel, Word, and Outlook. Demonstrated experience independently resolving problems and exercising judgment regarding administrative details and procedures. Demonstrated experience working with multiple levels of employees, and internal and external offices. Demonstrated organizational skills, including experience prioritizing, multi-tasking, and following-through on tasks. Experience coordinating administrative, personnel, academic, or operational processes involving multiple stakeholders and competing deadlines. Experience with scheduling meetings and events. Demonstrated written and oral communication skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience working in higher education. One year or more experience in an office involving faculty support. Experience with recruitment and hiring. Experience with online travel booking and expense systems. Knowledge of faculty promotion, tenure, and reappointment procedures. Experience working in a healthcare, medical, or pharmacy setting or academic environment. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $53,943 to $70,126. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA) and look for information specifically identified for UCPEA, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This position is a full-time, 12-month, permanent position. The selected incumbent must have the ability to work on occasional nights and weekends. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499879 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on November 11, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 28 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 11 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499853/maintenance-supervisor-1-plumbing-1st-shift-mf-limited-to-uconn-np2-employees-only-one-position": {
      "title": "Maintenance Supervisor 1 - Plumbing (1st Shift M-F) Limited to UConn NP-2 Employees Only (one position)",
      "description": "Maintenance Supervisor 1 - Plumbing (1st Shift M-F) Limited to UConn NP-2 Employees Only (one position) Apply now Search #: 499853 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Maintenance and Trades Maintenance Supervisor 1 – Plumbing 1 st Shift; Monday – Friday, 7:00 a.m. - 3:00 p.m. University of Connecticut, Facilities Operations, Storrs Campus Salary Range: $74,028 to $96,813 Yearly. Individuals new to state service start at the minimum of the pay plan. Link to Maintenance Supervisor 1 – Plumbing Job Specification This position is categorized as “Open to Agency Only” This position is open to current UConn NP-2 employees only INTRODUCTION UConn’s Facilities Operations Department has a job opening for a Maintenance Supervisor 1 – Plumbing position within the Plumbing Shop. This is a permanent, full-time, 1st shift position, with a work schedule of Monday – Friday, 7:00 a.m. - 3:00 p.m., 37.50 hours per week with an unpaid half an hour for lunch. The hourly rate of pay for current NP-2 employees is calculated based on current salary/step. This position is categorized as Emergency Support Services/Essential Staff. Must be willing to work during emergency closures/delayed openings due to inclement weather or for significant events that affect campus operations. Applicants must also be willing to respond to emergencies on overtime (nights, weekends, holidays and weather/storm related events). Please note that Facilities Operations NP-2 employees may also be required to perform job duties as assigned at any of the Regional Campuses. Additionally, this position may require participation in the Weekly On-Call Rotation Schedule; when assigned to the on-call rotation, the assigned cell phone must be carried, and calls must be responded to during the on-call assignment within 15 minutes. SELECTION PLAN This position is categorized as “Open to Agency Only”. Current, qualified UConn NP-2 employees will be considered for this position. This position requires considerable knowledge of modern methods and equipment involved in the plumbing trade. A current State of Connecticut P-1 or P-2 license is required and must be retained throughout employment with the University. A current motor vehicle operator license is required and must be retained throughout employment with the University. To Apply: In order to be considered for this position, you must meet the minimum qualifications as listed on the job posting and must specify your qualifications on your application. UConn Jobs applications will be evaluated to ensure that the Knowledge, Skill and Ability, Minimum Qualifications and Special Requirements sections of the position have been met. Please also be sure to demonstrate how you meet any of the Preferred Qualifications that you may have. Ensure that your UConn Jobs Application is complete and detailed before submitting it. You will not be able to make revisions once your application is submitted into the PageUp system. If applicable, a pplicants are strongly encouraged to indicate on their application (under the Job-Related Academic, Technical or Vocational Training section of the application) the name(s) of any Educational Institution(s) attended in trade related subjects, particularly in Plumbing and Heating, and to indicate the title(s) of the in person and/or online course(s) studied, Address, City, State where the Educational Institution(s) are located, the years attended and the number of Credits earned or Diploma Earned. If applicable, applicants are strongly encouraged to indicate on their application (under the Licenses and Professional Certification section of the application) any current trade license(s) held, along with the issuing agency, license number, date issued and expiration date . Applicants are required to indicate on their application (under the Licenses and Certification section of the application) their current driver’s license number, date issued and expiration date. Although it is not required, applicants are strongly encouraged to submit a cover letter, resume and a list of three professional, work-related references along with their contact information. Important Next Steps Information: At any point during the recruitment process, applicants may be contacted via email and provided with an opportunity to submit additional documentation which support their qualification(s) for this position. These documents may include: a cover letter, resume, performance reviews, attendance records, supervisory references, licensure, etc., at the discretion of the hiring department. Applicants must meet the minimum qualifications as indicated to apply for this position. If selected for an interview, you will be asked to supply the names, titles and phone numbers of three professional, work related references. In addition, current State of Connecticut employees at UConn or other State Agencies, may be asked to provide copies of their last two (2) performance appraisals and CORE attendance records. Please regularly check your email provided on your application for notifications. Please check your SPAM and/or Junk folders on a daily basis in the event an email provider places auto-notification emails in a user's spam. EXAMPLES OF DUTIES At UConn the Maintenance Supervisor 1 (MS1) – Plumbing position for the Facilities Operations Plumbing Shop reports to the Maintenance Supervisor 2 (MS2) – Plumbing, assists the MS2 – Plumbing with the following job duties and performs these job duties independently in the MS2 – Plumbing’s absence; this position also acts as a working supervisor and instructs staff as an “expert” in the Plumbing trade; the MS1 – Plumbing may also be required to perform the more technical tasks of the trade: responsible for leading and supervising highly skilled trades workers and apprentices in the repair and maintenance of plumbing systems; coordinating the daily work assignments of the Plumbing Shop; overseeing the purchase of tools and materials for all jobs and projects; working with the MS2 – Plumbing to maintain work schedules, job assignments and time records; maintaining employee records and handling disciplinary issues as required; completing service ratings, completing overtime equalization rosters, serving on hiring teams as a search committee member, overseeing areas of the high pressure hot water and steam systems on campus; working with the Utilities and HVACR Shops to ensure shared equipment is operating appropriately; reading and interpreting mechanical blueprints for building and utility systems; inspecting jobs for quality, as an “expert” in plumbing and steam fitting code requirements; bringing in and overseeing plumbing and steam fitting contractors when needed to upgrade and install new water and steam piping systems; working with engineering groups to plan upgrades and expansions of systems to accommodate growth of the campus; overseeing the estimating of projects and helping to set up assistance from other trade shops as needed; working with customers to determine work schedules, deciding when work can be done most efficiently, utilizing contractors when needed; assisting the management team in setting up budgets and long range goal planning; attending project meetings and making recommendations as to what standards for materials should be, along with determining the best method to carry out projects; monitoring cost tracking of projects to meet estimated cost; working with EHS to maintain safe and code compliant plumbing systems and work programs; working with the Business Service Center to schedule EHS and UConn training for the Plumbing Shop which includes but is not limited to: Continuing Education, Code, Asbestos Awareness, Confined Space, Lockout/Tag out, Mold Awareness, and Respiratory Protection & Fit Testing; responding to emergency plumbing failures outside of regular work hours as required. Schedules, assigns, oversees and reviews the work of staff; Provides staff training and assistance; Conducts performance evaluations; Determines priorities and plans crew work; Establishes and maintains crew procedures; Develops or makes recommendations on the development of policies and standards; Acts as liaison with other operating units, agencies and outside officials regarding unit policies and procedures; Prepares reports and correspondence; Performs some of the more skilled duties involved; May estimate time, skills and material needed for proposed repairs or improvements; Performs related duties as required. KNOWLEDGE, SKILL AND ABILITY Considerable knowledge of relevant state and national codes and regulations; modern methods and equipment involved in the trade indicated by the parenthetical title of the class; Skills interpersonal skills; oral and written communications skills; in performing technical duties of the class; Ability to read and interpret specifications, blueprints, and sketches; utilize computer software; Supervisory ability. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS - GENERAL EXPERIENCE Five (5) years of experience in the trade area indicated by the parenthetical title. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS - SPECIAL EXPERIENCE Two (2) years of the General Experience must have included performing highly skilled duties in the trade area indicated by the parenthetical title. NOTE: For state employees this experience is interpreted at the level of a Qualified Craft Worker. SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS Incumbents in this class may be required by the appointing authority to possess and retain appropriate current licenses, permits and/or certifications including possession and retention of a license as an Unlimited Journeyperson in the trade area indicated by the parenthetical title. Incumbents in this class may be required to travel. Incumbents in this class must have a current State of Connecticut P-1 or P-2 License. Incumbents must possess and retain a current motor vehicle operator license throughout employment with the University. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience serving in a leadership capacity that includes demonstrated experience in the supervision of employees serving in a wide range of skilled job classifications such as Student Labor Apprentices, Skilled Trades Apprentices and Quality Craft Workers (QCW’s). Eight years of plumbing trade experience. Experience working with HVACR and Utilities trade supervisors within the department to demonstrate knowledge, skill and ability in ensuring that shared equipment and systems are operating appropriately. Experience communicating effectively through both verbal and written methods. Experience using computer software such as AiM, HuskyBuy, Microsoft Excel, Word, Outlook, and/or Kuali Financial System (KFS). Demonstrated experience in the use of computerized maintenance management software (CMMS) including the use in the automation of such functions as service request/work order processing and work scheduling. Experience using an I-Pad or similar technology. Experience serving as a job search committee member to evaluate application materials and conduct interviews. Demonstrated experience in completing employee work performance evaluations. Experience completing purchasing related tasks such as obtaining price quotes, reviewing and approving invoices and/or participation in writing RFP’s (Request for Proposal) in the Plumbing field of work. Knowledge of the management and removal of regulated materials as well as knowledge of applicable laws, regulations, nationally recognized standards and guidelines, as related to occupational safety and health. Familiarity with Connecticut Public Health Code Regulations and Connecticut DEEP Regulations. Knowledgeable of the NP-2 contract and University policies and procedures. Experience working with the university payroll system CORE-CT. Experience working extended hours for plumbing related emergencies. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS Incumbents in this class must have adequate physical strength, stamina, physical agility and visual and auditory acuity, and must maintain such physical fitness as to be able to perform the duties of the class. A physical examination may be required. Incumbents in this class will be required to drive and operate motor vehicles, including, but not limited to vans, box trucks, and pick-up trucks ranging in size from compact, midsize, full-size, and heavy-duty, some of which are equipped with lift gates. Incumbents must have adequate physical strength and agility to walk up and down stairs frequently and climb ladders independently. Must be able to lift, carry and push material weighing up to 75 lbs. Must have the ability to utilize ladders, scaffolding, high-lifts and enter confined spaces. Must be able to inspect, troubleshoot and repair equipment that is part of a wide range of plumbing and mechanical systems. Incumbents must possess the ability to utilize tools, perform the required duties set forth above and will be required to wear personal protective equipment (PPE) when necessary. WORKING CONDITIONS Incumbents in this class may be required to lift moderate to heavy weights; may be exposed to extreme weather conditions and to risk of injury from equipment. The appointing authority may require completion of an asbestos removal program consistent with EPA guidelines for operations and maintenance during employment in this class. Incumbents may be required to use protective equipment such as respirators and safety goggles. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check and a pre-employment physical. APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499853. Although it is not required, applicants are strongly encouraged to submit a resume, cover letter , and a list of three professional, work-related references along with their contact information. Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is anticipated to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on November 13, 2026. CONCLUSION All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . Information concerning the University’s policies regarding the Cleary Act Statement, Non-Discrimination Policy, Accommodations for Applicants with Disabilities, and Recruiting Veterans can be found on UConn’s Job page. Please review the required notices and policy statements . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression. The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 28 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 13 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499885/administrative-program-support-2": {
      "title": "Administrative Program Support 2",
      "description": "Administrative Program Support 2 Apply now Search #: 499885 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Administrative Services JOB SUMMARY Provides administrative support for Information Technology Services (ITS) Business Office operations. Serves as a resource for ITS staff, student employees, vendors, and university departments to support efficient, accurate, and compliant business operations. Administers and manages student labor for department, including the posting, processing and payroll for all ITS departments. Assists in compiling departmental financial activities to be used for budgetary monitoring and fiscal projections by ITS Business Office Manager. Reporting Structure Works under the general supervision of the ITS Business Office Manager and collaborates with ITS leadership, staff, student employees, vendors, Human Resources, Payroll, and university departments. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Administrative Responsibilities Supports ITS projects by monitoring financial activity, maintaining project records, and ensuring appropriate cost allocations. Compiles data and prepares reports used to monitor departmental expenditures, budgets, projects, and services. Processes purchase requisitions, purchase orders, and other procurement-related transactions. Reviews vendor invoices for accuracy and facilitates payment processing in accordance with University procedures. Serve as a resource for ITS staff, university departments, student employees, and vendors on administrative, procurement, payroll, and financial processes. Assists with tracking software agreements, subscriptions, maintenance renewals, and related documentation. Provides backup administrative support as needed, including coordinating and scheduling ITS Leadership meetings in collaboration with the administrative team. Performs related duties as required. Student Labor/HR Functions Coordinates student employee hiring, onboarding, and offboarding, including hiring documentation, employment transactions, system access requests, and records management. Supports student payroll administration by reviewing timesheets, monitoring deadlines, resolving discrepancies, and coordinating with Human Resources and Payroll as needed. Maintains student employment records and tracks funding allocations, work schedules, appointment dates, and earnings to support planning and compliance with university requirements. Guides supervisors and student employees on hiring procedures, payroll processing, employment policies, and administrative requirements. Monitors student employee appointments to ensure extensions, renewals, position changes, and terminations are processed timely. Maintains departmental records, files, and documentation in accordance with university policies and procedures. Maintains knowledge of university policies, procedures, financial systems, payroll requirements, and business office processes; provides guidance and outreach as appropriate. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in business administration, accounting, finance, human resources, or a related field and at least two years of related professional experience. An equivalent combination of education and experience may be substituted for the degree and experience requirements. Experience performing administrative, financial, payroll, procurement, human resources, or business-office functions. Experience reviewing or reconciling financial or payroll transactions and researching or resolving discrepancies. Experience compiling, maintaining, and reviewing data, records, or reports. Experience using Microsoft Office applications, including Excel, Word, Outlook, and Teams, in a professional setting. Experience coordinating multiple administrative activities or processes with established deadlines. Experience preparing professional correspondence, reports, procedural instructions, or other administrative communications. Experience serving as a point of contact for employees, supervisors, vendors, customers, or organizational departments. Experience maintaining employee, payroll, financial, or other confidential records. Experience independently coordinating administrative processes involving multiple individuals, departments, or organizational units. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience supporting business or administrative operations in a higher education, public-sector, or similarly complex organization. Experience administering student employment processes, including hiring, onboarding, payroll review and finalization, appointment changes, and offboarding. Experience processing procurement transactions, reviewing invoices, and coordinating vendor payments. Experience monitoring budgets, reconciling financial activity, tracking expenditures, and preparing financial or operational reports. Experience with Core-CT, HuskyBuy, Kuali Financial Systems, or comparable enterprise financial, payroll, and procurement systems. Experience with Kuali Build or a comparable workflow and forms-management system. Advanced proficiency with Microsoft 365 applications, particularly Excel, Teams, Outlook, and SharePoint. Familiarity with basic accounting principles, cost allocations, and financial-reporting practices. Experience interpreting and communicating organizational policies, procedures, and administrative requirements. Demonstrated problem-solving skills and the ability to identify and resolve payroll, financial, procurement, or administrative discrepancies. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $53,943 to $70,126. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA) and look for information specifically identified for UCPEA, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499885 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on November 23, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 23 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 23 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499865/university-educational-assistant-3-uea3-executive-assistant-to-the-executive-leadership-team": {
      "title": "University Educational Assistant 3 (UEA3) - Executive Assistant to the Executive Leadership Team",
      "description": "University Educational Assistant 3 (UEA3) - Executive Assistant to the Executive Leadership Team Apply now Search #: 499865 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Administrative Services JOB SUMMARY Provides confidential executive, administrative, and operational support to the Vice President (VP) of Facility Services and University Planning (FSUP), as well as executive-level support to the Associate Vice President (AVP) of Facility Operations (FO), the Executive Director of University Planning, Design and Construction (UPDC), and other members of the FSUP executive leadership team, as assigned. This is a confidential exempt position, with dual emphasis on managing the schedule and activities of the VP of FSUP, as well as coordinating the day-to-day administrative activities under the direction of the VP of FSUP, while maintaining the highest level of confidentiality. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Serves as executive assistant and logistical point of contact for the Vice President (VP) of Facility Services and University Planning (FSUP). Coordinates a complex schedule of activities, coordinates meetings, travel arrangements, financial reimbursements, and logistics for the VP of FSUP, AVP of FO, and Executive Director of UPDC. Ensures that the VP and other executive leaders are fully prepared to participate in all scheduled meetings and activities. Maintains strict confidentiality while handling sensitive information, executive communications, and labor and administrative matters, regularly exercising discretion in the conduct of business and administrative activities. Handles a large volume of incoming communications and responds to inquiries from internal and external stakeholders on behalf of the VP of FSUP on routine matters and as directed. Conducts inquiries and research, reviews and summarizes information, drafts documents and professional correspondence for executive review. Schedules and coordinates recurring contractor business reviews and other departmental meetings, including preparing agendas, records and documents meeting minutes and notes, and tracks follow-up action items. Maintains complex day-to-day prioritization and management of the FSUP department calendars, monitors scheduling conflicts, and coordinates priorities across multiple areas. Assists in department initiatives, administrative processes, and special projects to support department and university goals. Plans and coordinates relevant logistics such as organizing agendas, scheduling and setting up meeting rooms, and managing catering for office-related events, meetings and departmental functions. Manages daily office operations by maintaining office supply inventory, distributes department mail, verifies department website information, and provides administrative support to ensure efficient workflow. Administer SharePoint file access permissions and coordinates software application and license requests and/or renewals to support departmental operations. Performs related duties as required. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Associate’s degree and three years of experience supporting the operations of an office, supporting management or leadership, or in a related administrative capacity. Education and training may be substituted for years of experience. Demonstrated analytical and interpersonal skills, with ability to negotiate and resolve problems of unusual difficulty. Experience providing executive-level administrative support and managing a complex schedule of executive activities or equivalent. Demonstrated ability to maintain strict confidentiality and to function effectively in ambiguous situations requiring the exercise of significant judgment and discretion. Demonstrated written and verbal communication skills. Experience with Microsoft Office Suite and its associated applications such as Outlook, Word, and excel, as well as PDF platforms and other scheduling tools. Demonstrated attention to detail in composing and formatting documents and spreadsheets. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree and one to two years of experience providing administrative support in a fast-paced business office within a large, complex organization. Two or more years of experience providing executive/administrative support in a higher education administrative office or similarly complex organization. Experience reviewing and improving administrative and internal operating procedures, performing analysis, interpreting and summarizing findings, and making appropriate recommendations; drafting correspondence, policies, reports, memoranda. Experience using Nitro Sign to administer electronic signature workflows for sensitive information and confidential business documents. Demonstrated knowledge of UConn administrative and fiscal policies and procedures. Experience using University enterprise systems, including Kuali Financial System (KFS), Core-CT, Concur, HuskyBuy, or similar enterprise applications. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $62,000 to $75,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is considered management/confidential and look for information specifically identified for management/confidential, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a 40 hour per week, full-time, 12-month permanent position. This position will work 8:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m., with a one-hour lunch, to provide coverage during the University's standard business hours. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. This position is categorized as Emergency Support Services/Essential Staff. Candidates must be willing and able to work during emergency closures/delayed openings due to inclement weather or for significant events that affect campus operations. Must have a willingness and ability to work evenings and weekends. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499865 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on November 30, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 23 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 30 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499843/assistant-associate-or-full-professor-quantum-science": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor, Quantum Science",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor, Quantum Science Apply now Search #: 499843 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Faculty Tenure Track JOB SUMMARY The Department of Mathematics and the Department of Statistics at the University of Connecticut (UConn) jointly invite applications for a full-time, tenure-track or tenured position at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor or Professor in quantum science. This position is part of a multi-departmental cluster hire in quantum science—the first phase of a \"Quantum Futures Cluster\"— supporting UConn’s leadership in the NSF Quantum Technologies Engine in Connecticut (QuantumCT Engine). The hire is central to a major, long-term strategic investment to establish Connecticut as a national leader in quantum technology. To strengthen the research and educational missions identified in the strategic plans of the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences and the University, we seek a mathematician or statistician with expertise in quantum algorithms, quantum information science, optimization, and post-quantum cryptography to advance the goals of the QuantumCT Engine and create natural synergies with AI for ImpaCT, a university-wide initiative to drive AI innovations across Connecticut. We invite applications from candidates across a broad spectrum of quantum-related research in Mathematics or Statistics. The successful candidate will contribute to the development of next-generation quantum methodologies and help shape the future of mathematics and statistics in the quantum era. The QuantumCT Engine aims to advance American quantum innovation and secure the domestic quantum supply chain by accelerating the commercialization of quantum technologies for national defense, biotechnology and financial services. Through innovation, applied research leading to new technologies, support for inventors and entrepreneurs and workforce development, the QuantumCT Engine will advance quantum sensing, secure communications, computing, and materials through a shared testbed, deep-tech incubator, and translation pathways. Led by the Division of Academic Affairs and the Provost’s Office, the AI for ImpaCT initiative is focused on advancing artificial intelligence best practices across education, research, innovation, public engagement, and workforce development. UConn brings together expertise from across disciplines to help students, employers, communities, and policymakers understand both the opportunities and challenges presented by AI. The Department of Mathematics and the Department of Statistics at UConn are both highly active centers of research, education, and service with regional, national, and international prestige. The successful candidate will have a primary appointment in either department depending on their preference, with affiliation to the other, and will play a leadership role in UConn's growing quantum research ecosystem. Founded in 1881, UConn is a Land Grant and Sea Grant institution and member of the Space Grant Consortium. It is the state's flagship institution of higher education and includes a main campus in Storrs, CT, four regional campuses throughout the state, and 14 Schools and Colleges, including a Law School in Hartford, and Medical and Dental Schools at the UConn Health campus in Farmington. The University has roughly over 10,000 faculty and staff and 34,000 students, including nearly 26,000 undergraduates and over 8,000 graduate and professional students. UConn is a Carnegie Foundation R1 (highest research activity) institution, among the top 25 public universities in the nation. Through research, teaching, service, and outreach, UConn embraces diversity and cultivates leadership, integrity, and engaged citizenship in its students, faculty, staff, and alumni. UConn promotes the health and well-being of citizens by enhancing the social, economic, cultural, and natural environments of the state and beyond. The University serves as a beacon of academic and research excellence as well as a center for innovation and social service to communities. UConn is a leader in many scholarly, research, and innovation areas. Today, the path forward includes exciting opportunities and notable challenges. Record numbers of undergraduate applications and support for student success have enabled the University to become extraordinarily selective. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Conducting high-level, original research in the intersection of quantum science and mathematics/statistics. Seeking external funding. Supervising PhD students and mentoring postdoctoral scholars. Teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in mathematics or statistics and related quantum disciplines. Service to the departments, the university, and the broader professional mathematical and statistical community. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Ph.D. degree in mathematical sciences, statistics, or closely related disciplines. A distinguished record of academic accomplishment in one or more of the targeted areas for this position. A strong record of securing external research funding from federal agencies and/or industry partners. Ability to teach core courses in the appointed department(s) at both undergraduate and graduate levels. Demonstrated interpersonal and communication skills. Potential to contribute significantly to the QuantumCT project at UConn in building and sustaining a vibrant research program in quantum science. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Potential to interact productively with researchers working in the priority areas of the University of Connecticut and its College of Arts and Sciences such as artificial intelligence and other interdisciplinary fields. Demonstrated commitment to professional service and public engagement. COMPENSATION & BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $125,000 to $240,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves and Engagement & Learning headers at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the American Association of University Professors (AAUP) bargaining unit and look for information specifically identified as AAUP, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track or tenured position with an anticipated start date of August 23, 2027. The successful candidate’s academic appointment will be at the Storrs campus. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the American Association of University Professors UConn Chapter (AAUP). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499843 and submit the following application materials: A cover letter (candidates should clearly specify their target rank as either Assistant, Associate or Full Professor); Curriculum vitae; Research and Scholarship statement (innovative concepts that will form the basis of academic career, experience in proposal development, mentorship of graduate students, etc.); Teaching statement (including teaching philosophy, teaching experience, commitment to effective learning, concepts for new course development, etc.); Name and Contact information for 3 professional reference s. Review of applications will begin immediately. Full consideration will be given to applications completed and received before December 1, 2026. For more information, please visit the Department of Math and the Department of Statistics websites. Inquiries can be addressed to the Search Committee Co-Chairs, Vasileios Chousionis or Ming-Hui Chen. At the University of Connecticut, our commitment to excellence is complemented by our commitment to building a culturally diverse community. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on December 22, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 23 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Dec 22 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499191/financial-aid-officer-processing-unit": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Officer (Processing Unit)",
      "description": "Financial Aid Officer (Processing Unit) Apply now Search #: 499191 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Student Programs and Services JOB SUMMARY The Office of Student Financial Aid Services is seeking an organized, detail-oriented, self-motivated Financial Aid Officer. This position is responsible for performing all aspects of financial aid processing, including need analysis, determining aid eligibility, completing professional judgments, and coordinating several key processing areas, such as census date aid adjustments. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Assists with all aspects of financial aid processing, including reviewing files and documentation, conducting need analysis and packaging, completing queries to ensure accurate aid offers, processing scholarships, and special projects. Responsible for the interpretation of and compliance with Financial Aid regulations governing respective program, formulation of policy guidelines and procedures, and helps to resolve complex problems in one or more areas of specialization. Participates in various outreach activities by serving as a financial aid representative at university open house(s) and other outreach activities. Maintains up-to-date knowledge of federal, state, and institutional rules, regulations, statutes, and mandates governing financial aid, and shares updates with the department that may affect areas of primary and secondary responsibility. Performs other related duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree from a college or university accredited by the US Department of Education or internationally recognized accrediting organization. Three years of related experience. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Three years progressively responsible financial aid experience. Preference will be given to candidates with experience using an automated financial aid system (e.g. PeopleSoft, Banner, etc.). Knowledge of federal and state regulations that govern the administration of financial aid programs. Demonstrated knowledge of financial aid processes (e.g., FAFSA, Verification, need analysis and packaging). Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Independent research and problem-solving skills. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $58,942 to $76,625. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA) and look for information specifically identified for UCPEA, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, twelve-month position. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499191 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on November 23, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 20 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 23 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499829/assistant-director-individualized-and-interdisciplinary-studies-program": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Individualized and Interdisciplinary Studies Program",
      "description": "Assistant Director, Individualized and Interdisciplinary Studies Program Apply now Search #: 499829 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Student Programs and Services JOB SUMMARY Reporting to the Director of the Individualized and Interdisciplinary Studies Program (IISP), the Assistant Director will develop and deliver program content for the Individualized Major (IMJR) Program, University Scholar Program, and several interdisciplinary minors. The core of the Assistant Director’s work will involve developing and coordinating program content and delivery; advising pre- and current individualized majors; collaborating with faculty and academic administrators; leading and coordinating committee meetings and projects; monitoring student progress toward degree completion; and coordinating a wide range of services and activities for students served by the office’s programs. The Assistant Director will be a highly experienced higher education professional, well-informed about the range of undergraduate courses and programs available at the University. The Assistant Director will also be an effective problem solver, accustomed to working independently, and capable of managing academically oriented administrative tasks. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Serves as the “second in command” for the Individualized and Interdisciplinary Studies Program (IISP). Develops, plans, and coordinates efforts towards establishing new programs, including leading various committees dedicated to developing structured major curriculum requirements and assessing program rigor. Serves as an instructor of the IMJR Gateway course for students interested in the Individualized Major Program; provides support to IISP Director on other IMJR-specific courses and curriculum. Administers the Individualized and Interdisciplinary Studies Program (IISP) application cycles throughout the academic year, including student outreach and recruitment and coordination of application and selection processes. Assists the Director in leading the expansion of IISP and IMJR programs on the regional campuses, assisting with developing strategic plans for addressing individual campus needs in collaboration with campus leadership and faculty. Advises students in developing individualized plans of study; may advise regarding alternative options for majors, complementary plans of study, and consistency with graduate school and career goals. Coordinates programs and events for IMJR students and collaborates with campus offices on joint programming efforts. Maintains appropriate files and records in order to compile and generate statistical reports related to program effectiveness and audit student records for IMJRs and IISP-administered minors to determine progress toward meeting academic requirements and to assess eligibility to graduate. Assists Director in planning and implementing scheduling and registration activities, orientation programs, and other activities, as assigned. Serves as a primary resource for students, faculty, parents, and others with questions about programs or academic policies; make referrals as appropriate. Assists in writing and/or editing informational and publicity materials for print, website, and social media platforms. Serves as communications liaison with IMJR Admissions Committee and faculty advisors, providing them with regular updates on student progress. Assists in the supervision of IISP student employees, interns, and/or peer advisors. Serves as a liaison with other university offices and external partners to provide enrichment opportunities – such as education abroad, internships, research, and professional development – for students served by IISP. Represents IISP and/or Enrichment Programs at UConn events, which may require occasional evening and weekend hours. Complete other duties, as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree in counseling, education, or relevant academic field AND four (4) years’ experience working with undergraduate students in a professional advising, counseling role, and/or teaching role. Two (2) years of experience must be serving as an advanced or lead team member. Demonstrated experience planning, coordinating, and implementing program services and activities. Demonstrated written and oral communication skills. Experience with interdisciplinary learning or research. Demonstrated organizational skills and demonstrated ability to manage multiple short- and long-term projects, including the proven ability to manage and perform administrative functions. Experience working with diverse, underrepresented, and/or first-generation students. Demonstrated ability to successfully advise undergraduate students and to interpret educational records and data related to student academic performance and progress. Experience compiling data and generating reports. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Doctoral degree. A degree in an interdisciplinary field. Professional work experience at a large university. Demonstrated experience prioritizing diversity, equity, and inclusion in practice. Experience teaching at the undergraduate level. Experience with supervision of student staff. Demonstrated ability to troubleshoot problems that interfere with students' academic progress and experience in working to resolve them effectively. Experience with assessment and program evaluation. Experience with database management. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $76,000 to $83,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA) and look for information specifically identified for UCPEA, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, permanent position. The University offers a competitive salary, and outstanding benefits , including employee and dependent tuition waivers at UConn, and a highly desirable work environment. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499829 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . In your cover letter, please explain how you meet the minimum qualifications of the position as well as any preferred qualifications. Please note that the committee will not contact references until later in the search process, after discussion with applicants. Incomplete applications will NOT be considered. Applicants should include the search number in all correspondence. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on November 6, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 16 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 06 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499864/secure-research-team-lead": {
      "title": "Secure Research Team Lead",
      "description": "Secure Research Team Lead Apply now Search #: 499864 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Information Technology JOB SUMMARY The University of Connecticut (UConn) Information Technology Services (ITS) is seeking an IT Team Lead 2 to manage IT professionals and lead technical operations within the Secure Research environment. Working under the general direction of a manager or director, this position oversees and provides advanced technical analysis, design, deployment, and ongoing management of Microsoft 365, Microsoft Entra, and related Azure services. The position establishes team priorities and workflows, supervises and develops staff, and maintains accountability for the availability and continuity of assigned services supporting secure research. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Team Supervision and Development: Schedule, assign, oversee, and review the work of IT staff responsible for installing, upgrading, maintaining, and supporting Secure Research technology. Provide staff training, technical guidance, and assistance to promote effective service delivery and staff development. Team Planning and Service Coordination: Determine team priorities, plan workloads, and establish and maintain unit workflows. Receive and prioritize technical requests, assign tasks based on staff expertise and capacity, and monitor progress, quality, and completion against project and service objectives. Microsoft 365 Service Management: Oversee and perform the configuration, administration, and support of Exchange Online, SharePoint, Teams, and other Microsoft 365 services in a secure, Azure-based research environment. Assess technical requirements and recommend solutions that meet researcher and institutional needs. Microsoft Entra Administration: Lead the implementation, maintenance, and enforcement of identity and access management policies, user and group administration, role-based access controls, and conditional access policies. Guide staff in applying security best practices. Azure Government and Technical Strategy: Maintain current knowledge of Azure Government cloud features and secure research practices. Evaluate emerging Microsoft and Azure technologies, assess their suitability, and recommend strategies and improvements for the Secure Research environment. System Implementation and Upgrades: Plan and oversee new system implementations and upgrades to applications, systems, networks, and hardware supporting assigned services. Coordinate staff activities, testing, and rollout with other IT teams to meet division goals and minimize service disruption. Service Availability and Problem Resolution: Maintain accountability for the availability and continuity of assigned services and systems. Work with IT staff to identify and resolve system errors, coordinate escalations and service restoration, and implement improvements that reduce recurring issues. Documentation and Process Improvement: Develop, maintain, and review technical documentation, standard operating procedures, security policies, and system diagrams. Establish procedures and techniques to analyze and improve the performance and efficiency of supported software and hardware systems. Collaboration and Vendor Coordination: Serve as a liaison among the Secure Research team, researchers, other IT departments, and external customers. Coordinate work across teams, communicate priorities and service issues, and direct staff collaboration with vendors and management to develop or enhance products and services. Information Security: Support the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of University information as part of the University Information Security Program. Incorporate applicable security requirements into team workflows, system administration, and service delivery. Additional Duties: Perform related work and projects as assigned to support the broader Secure Research Infrastructure. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Associate’s degree plus eight (8) years of related experience; OR Bachelor’s degree plus six (6) years of related experience; OR Ten (10) years of related experience. AND one (1) year of experience serving as a supervisor of a moderate-sized department or leading major projects. Microsoft 365 Expertise: Proven hands-on experience configuring, administering, and supporting Microsoft 365 services, including Exchange Online, SharePoint, and Teams. Microsoft Entra and Access Control: Experience with Microsoft Entra user administration, role-based access control, conditional access policies, and security best practices; working knowledge of Active Directory users and groups or Azure RBAC roles. Technical Knowledge: Proven in-depth technical knowledge and the ability to design and support IT services in a multi-tiered environment, including knowledge of systems analysis, design, development, and programming/scripting. Planning and Problem Solving: Demonstrated ability to plan, prioritize, and organize team assignments and technical projects; apply troubleshooting, critical thinking, and analysis to resolve problems; and use common productivity software such as Microsoft Word, Excel, and Visio. Communication and Teamwork: Demonstrated ability to provide technical training and guidance to peers and less technical users, apply active listening, communicate effectively orally and in writing, and maintain effective working relationships with individuals of diverse cultural, social, and educational backgrounds. Documentation and Professional Practice: Demonstrated ability to prepare clear procedures, processes, and physical and logical network diagrams; investigate and apply new skills and technologies; and maintain confidentiality and professional boundaries. US Citizenship: Required due to security regulations. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Regulatory Compliance: Experience working with Controlled Unclassified Information (CUI), NIST SP 800-171, and/or CMMC 2.0. Azure Virtual Desktop: Demonstrated familiarity with provisioning and managing virtual desktop infrastructure in Azure. Microsoft Azure Administration: Hands-on experience deploying virtual machines, storage accounts, creating resource groups, and managing Azure subscriptions. Network & Security: Demonstrated understanding of firewall policies, Azure Network Security Groups, and general network configuration (e.g., virtual networks, subnets, public/private endpoints). OS Administration: Experience managing Windows and/or Linux servers in a production environment. Scripting and Automation: Proven proficiency with PowerShell, Ansible, Azure CLI, or other automation tools. Higher Education Experience: Previous experience in a higher education IT environment. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $95,066 to $123,585. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA) and look for information specifically identified for UCPEA, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499864 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on November 16, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 16 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 16 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499642/regional-campus-housing-coordinator": {
      "title": "Regional Campus Housing Coordinator",
      "description": "Regional Campus Housing Coordinator Apply now Search #: 499642 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Student Programs and Services JOB SUMMARY The Department of Housing Administration and Finance plays a vital role in the success of the University of Connecticut and its students by providing quality facilities, personnel, services and programs that contribute to the mission of the University. UConn Campus Housing enhances students’ personal, interpersonal, and intellectual growth by creating safe and inclusive environments, innovative programs, and fostering meaningful relationships. Our comprehensive, student-centered, residential program houses over 14,600 students across three campuses. Approximately 65% of the undergraduate population resides in university-managed housing. We offer our students an array of housing facilities and options to enhance their learning experience, including a variety of learning communities and special interest housing options. Campus Housing consists of two departments: Housing Administration and Finance, and Residential Living and Education. Housing Administration and Finance works cooperatively with Facilities Operations and many other campus partners and departments. The Regional Campus Housing Coordinator is responsible for the central coordination of housing assignment functions for the student population residing at regional campuses, including special-interest groups. Assist and support management of occupancy for on-campus housing through database management and constant communication with students, faculty, staff, and family members to ensure accurate record keeping for emergency, billing, and community-building needs. This position reports to the Assistant Director of Housing Administration and Finance. To be effective, this position needs to work in close collaboration with leaders and staff through Campus Housing, the regional campuses and the University. The regional campus housing coordinator will share time between all regional campuses and the Storrs campus, with a home base in Storrs. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as the liaison for the regional campuses to provide support for policy questions, processes and procedures for students. Responsible for assisting in the management and maintenance of residential occupancy data and administration of room assignment policies and procedures throughout the year for approximately 1000 students at regional campuses. Provide information and support to various specialty areas, including but not limited to: Center for Students with Disabilities, Student Health and Wellness, Learning Communities, Greek organizations, Veterans Affairs, Gender Inclusive, Athletics, and Honors. Maintain appropriate and accurate records and compile reports as needed or requested. Utilizes multiple computer programs for data entry and compilation of detailed spreadsheets. Provide individual support and advice to students as needed concerning housing assignments. This may include one-on-one discussions with students about their personal situations and roommate-related issues. Serve as primary contact for residence hall staff in relation to room and building changes for on-campus undergraduate students, utilizing developmental principles, ensuring policies are applied consistently, and independently making exceptions to policies when appropriate. Assist with the development of policies, procedures, publications, goal development, assessment activities, training activities, and overall operation of the housing services unit, specific to the operations of regional campuses. Independently compose, prepare and edit correspondence and reports containing information that requires thorough knowledge and understanding of housing policies and procedures; and assist in generating and developing communications for students, parents, and the University community related to on-campus housing policies and procedures. Serve as the first contact to students and parents in dealing with issues related to housing assignment concerns. Resolve these issues using sound judgment and creative decision-making. Respond to inquiries and resolve complaints from students, parents, staff and public regarding policies and procedures related to housing assignments. Inform current and prospective students and parents of policies and procedures and represent the department in communication. Send out mass communications to students on campus informing them of upcoming processes or policies that are in place. Process all transactions in the Housing software program related to moves in vacancies, trading spaces processes, and new students’ assignments. Present housing information to residence hall students, potential students, their families and other University staff. Including but not limited to Open House and Orientation. Assist with the periodic testing of housing application and selection processes and software upgrades. Create communications for students who are assigned to various areas of campus corresponding with the specialty groups listed above. Communicate with these students regarding their needs and assist them in finding assignments that meet these needs, including email, phone, and in-person meetings by coordinating with campus and community partners. Participate in various committees and work groups. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Minimum of two years of full-time post-Bachelor's degree experience in a higher education field. Demonstrated human relations and communication skills, with the ability to interact with students, families, and campus partners. Clear commitment to, demonstrated understanding of and experience working with diverse populations. Articulated knowledge of the trends related to college students, including organizational, personnel and fiscal management. Experience managing complex and detailed projects/tasks. Experience with housing assignment software. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience working in a large residential program. Master’s degree. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $60,416 to $63,416. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA) and look for information specifically identified for UCPEA, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, permanent position. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499642 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. The review of applications will begin immediately and this position will remain open until filled. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 16 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
